The training observations show that 0.2 % proof strengths above ~2000 MPa occur almost exclusively for quench-and-temper or solution-age treatments (annealed alloys are systematically weaker). Within those families the strongest examples combine moderate-to-high Co (4–7 wt %) with Ni, Cr, Mo/Nb/V and a high solutionizing temperature followed by a quench (cooling rate ≳ 30) and a subsequent age/temper of 10–40 h.  

Among the pool members that also satisfy Mo ≤ 3.21 wt % and alloying-cost ≤ 450 the five compositions that most closely reproduce this pattern are:

- c044 (quench-temper, cast, Co 4.72, Cr–Ni–Mo–Nb, T_sol = 1228 °C, t_age = 28 h) – nearest neighbour to the 2074 MPa training point;  
- c007 (quench-temper, wrought, Co 5.26, high Ni + Nb + Ti);  
- c029 (quenched, wrought, Co 4.86, Ni–V–Ti);  
- c068 (solution-aged, wrought, Co 4.58, Cr–Ni–Nb–Ti – analogue of the 1961 MPa AM alloy but with a wrought route);  
- c003 (quench-temper, cast, Co 4.38, Cr–Ni–Ti).

All other feasible candidates either lack the Co/Ni/Mo combination, use an annealed cycle, have an extremely short ageing time, or employ an AM route that, on the training evidence, sits 50–100 MPa lower.

Final: c044, c007, c029, c068, c003
